# Routh II.Art.146 — an impulsive couple, and what it does to the invariable
# line — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 146: "Let the body
# while in motion be acted on by an impulsive couple whose moment is I and
# whose plane is perpendicular to the resultant axis of angular momentum. To
# find the change in the motion."
#
# WHAT AN IMPULSIVE COUPLE DOES. It adds its whole moment to the angular
# momentum instantaneously and changes nothing else -- not the orientation, not
# the position. So L -> L + I, and everything about the new motion follows from
# the new L: the new invariable line, the new G, the new polhode.
#
# THE TWO CASES WORTH SEPARATING.
#
#   couple ALONG L (its plane perpendicular to L, Routh's case): the invariable
#       LINE is unchanged in direction. Only G changes. The body keeps the same
#       fixed axis in space and simply carries more angular momentum about it.
#
#   couple ACROSS L: the invariable line TILTS, through the angle
#       arctan(|I| / G), and the whole subsequent motion is referred to a new
#       fixed direction in space.
#
# This notebook does the second, because it is the one with a number to check,
# and states the first alongside it.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2), I = diag(5,
# 4.25, 1.25), tumbling at w = (0.3, 3, 0.1):
#
#     L = (1.5, 12.75, 0.125)        G = 12.838540610209558
#
# An impulsive couple of moment 5 is applied perpendicular to L, along
# L x zhat normalised:
#
#     J     = (4.965753021614381, -0.584206237836986, 0)
#     new L = (6.465753021614381, 12.165793762163014, 0.125)
#     |L'|  = 13.777812779973459   =  sqrt(G^2 + 25)   as it must be
#     tilt  = 21.278543789162757 degrees = arctan(5 / G)
#
# HOW IT IS APPLIED. posim has no impulse command, and does not need one: an
# impulsive couple IS an instantaneous change of angular momentum, so writing
# the new value straight into obj0.angular_momentum is exactly Routh's
# operation, not an approximation of it. The angular velocity that posim
# reports afterwards is recomputed from the new L through w = R I^-1 R^T L,
# which is the "change in the motion" the article asks for.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0 so nothing else acts.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a box tumbling about one fixed direction in space, then --
# at the moment the notebook rewrites its angular momentum -- tumbling about a
# different one, twenty-one degrees away.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_impulsive_couple
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_impulsive_couple.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_impulsive_couple.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

get obj0.angular_momentum
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
energy

# the impulsive couple: L -> L + J, with J perpendicular to L and |J| = 5
set obj0.angular_momentum = [6.465753021614381, 12.165793762163014, 0.125]

norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
# cosine of the tilt against the old invariable line: cos(21.2785 deg) = 0.9318...
dot(normalize(obj0.angular_momentum), normalize([1.5, 12.75, 0.125]))
# the angular velocity has been recomputed from the new L
get obj0.angular_velocity
energy

# and the new invariable line is fixed in space just as the old one was
run 4 steps 4
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.03
